@page "/menu"

<CatHead Title="Menus">
    One dropdown panel style, for a short list of <em>actions</em> opened from a control, and the
    value trigger that opens one. The header's user menu is the same <code>.menu</code> class
    &mdash; there is not a second panel style for the frame. A panel of <em>content</em> is a
    <a href="/popover">popover</a>.
</CatHead>

<div class="callout callout--warn">
    <i class="ri-error-warning-line"></i>
    <div class="callout-body">
        <span class="callout-title">Do not add <code>role="menu"</code></span>
        The script moves focus between the items with <kbd>&uarr;</kbd>, <kbd>&darr;</kbd>,
        <kbd>Home</kbd> and <kbd>End</kbd>, jumps to an item by typing its first letters, and opens a
        submenu with the arrow towards it. But the items stay ordinary links and buttons, each its own
        <kbd>Tab</kbd> stop &mdash; there is no roving tabindex &mdash; and a menu may hold a filter field.
        <code>role="menu"</code> promises one tab stop and children that are all
        <code>menuitem</code>s, and a screen reader stops announcing links as links inside it. Put
        <code>aria-expanded</code> on the trigger and leave the panel without a role.
    </div>
</div>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Menu.AnchoredUnderTrigger" Title="Anchored under a trigger" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <strong>Open it — the demo is live.</strong> <code>.menu-anchor</code> wraps the trigger and
        the panel and declares the <code>anchor-name</code> the panel positions against, so the
        panel lands under the trigger with nothing measured. <code>data-menu-toggle</code> on the
        trigger hands it to <code>sednaUi.menu</code> — one panel open at a time, a click outside or
        on an item closing it, <kbd>Escape</kbd> closing it and returning focus to the trigger — and
        the closed state is the <code>hidden</code> <strong>attribute</strong> rather than a class,
        which is what takes the panel out of the tab order as well as out of the layout; an app
        holding the state in C# leaves the attribute off and renders <code>hidden</code> itself. The
        panel lines up with the anchor's <em>trailing</em> edge and sizes to its widest row, floored
        at 220px — add <code>.menu--start</code> to the panel for a trigger near the leading edge.
        A menu is for actions: a list of <em>values</em> is a <code>&lt;select&gt;</code> or a
        <a href="/segmented">segmented control</a>, a list of <em>places</em> is the sidebar.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Menu.AtThePointer" Title="At the pointer, with a submenu" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <strong>Live — right-click a row.</strong> <code>data-menu-context="&lt;id&gt;"</code> on
        any element opens the <code>.menu</code> with that id and the <code>popover</code> attribute
        where the reader right-clicked, or below the element for the <kbd>ContextMenu</kbd> key and
        <kbd>Shift</kbd> <kbd>F10</kbd>; the script places an invisible <code>.menu-point</code>
        there and the panel flips at the screen's edges as a dropdown does. The element it opened on
        carries <code>data-menu-context-target</code> while it is open. A
        <code>.menu-sub-anchor</code> holds an item with <code>data-menu-sub</code> and the
        <code>.menu.menu-sub</code> it opens beside it. In every menu, <kbd>↑</kbd> <kbd>↓</kbd>
        <kbd>Home</kbd> <kbd>End</kbd> move between items and <kbd>→</kbd> <kbd>←</kbd> enter and
        leave a submenu.
    </p>
    <p>
        For a menu your own code decides to open, call
        <code>sednaUi.menu.openAt('order-menu', event.clientX, event.clientY, row)</code> &mdash;
        elements or ids &mdash; or <a href="/interop"><code>ISednaUi.OpenMenuAtAsync</code></a> from
        C#. It answers whether a menu opened. A scroll that moves the element it is about closes it,
        and one opened at a bare point closes on any scroll outside it.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Menu.Filter" Title="A menu that filters" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <strong>Live &mdash; open it and type.</strong> An <code>&lt;input data-menu-filter&gt;</code>
        with <code>.menu-filter</code> at the top of the panel narrows the items as the reader types,
        ranked by the same matcher as the command palette: an item that does not match is
        <code>hidden</code>, and so is a <code>.menu-label</code> with nothing left under it. Your
        <code>.menu-empty</code> row shows while nothing matches &mdash; the panel carries
        <code>data-menu-empty</code> then. The menu opens with the filter cleared and focused;
        <kbd>↓</kbd> moves to the first item and <kbd>↑</kbd> from it comes back, and
        <kbd>Enter</kbd> chooses the first item.
    </p>
    <p>
        Without a filter, a letter typed on an item moves to the next item whose label starts with
        it, and letters typed quickly together find a whole word &mdash; the label is
        <code>data-label</code> when the item carries one, else its text.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Menu.ValueTriggerInATable" Title="A value trigger in a table cell" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <strong>Live — open one, then scroll the table sideways.</strong>
        <code>.value-trigger</code> shows the <em>current value</em> of something and opens a menu to
        change it — a status, an assignee, an access level. Reach for it where a
        <code>&lt;select&gt;</code> would be wrong because the options need icons or a description;
        where a plain list of values would do, use a <code>&lt;select&gt;</code>. Mark the value it
        holds now with <code>aria-current="true"</code> on its item and say so in words, and name the
        trigger with its value as well as what it sets.
        <code>--value-trigger-measure</code> is how much of the value the control keeps room for,
        10ch by default, and <code>.col-control</code> on the <code>th</code> and the
        <code>td</code> gives the column its floor — see <a href="/table">Tables</a>.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<div class="callout callout--info">
    <i class="ri-smartphone-line"></i>
    <div class="callout-body">
        <span class="callout-title">On a phone, a sheet</span>
        Below 768px a dropdown and a menu at the pointer open as a bottom sheet: the full width of
        the screen, on its bottom edge and clear of the home indicator, with taller rows and the page
        above it dimmed. A press on the dimmed page closes the menu and lands on nothing under it; a
        submenu opens in place under its item. Nothing changes in the markup. The
        <a href="/button">speed dial</a> keeps its fan.
    </div>
</div>

<CatSnippet Name="Menu/Dismissing.txt" Title="Dismissing it">
    <p>
        There is no JavaScript behind this. Put <code>.menu-scrim</code> immediately
        <strong>before</strong> the panel — a transparent full-viewport element that catches the
        click which closes the menu, and coming first in the markup is what puts the panel above it.
        Handle <kbd>Escape</kbd> on the wrapper as well, or the menu can only be closed with a
        pointer.
    </p>
</CatSnippet>


